Export native rectangular wall openings (OST_SWallRectOpening) as proxies.

Openings placed with Revit's Opening tool were not a recovered category, so
the hole they cut never reached the export and their walls stayed whole
(three in a user's Revit 2025 model, 0.2 x 1.67 m and 0.3 x 1.91 m, each
record box exactly the wall's thickness). The category is now recovered
like a generic model and exported as an IfcBuildingElementProxy named
`WallOpening-<ElementId>` with its record box, for a consumer to cut from
the wall it crosses (the file importer's void_families.py does).

diff --git a/src/ifc/category_map.rs b/src/ifc/category_map.rs
index 863c1c8..797beeb 100644
--- a/src/ifc/category_map.rs
+++ b/src/ifc/category_map.rs
@@ -359,6 +359,13 @@ pub const MAPPINGS: &[Mapping] = &[
         ifc_type: "IFCBUILDINGELEMENTPROXY",
         predefined_type: None,
     },
+    // A native rectangular wall opening: its record box, as a proxy, for a
+    // consumer to cut from the wall it crosses.
+    Mapping {
+        revit_class: "WallOpening",
+        ifc_type: "IFCBUILDINGELEMENTPROXY",
+        predefined_type: None,
+    },
     // RE-37: the entities Revit's own IFC4 export writes for these
     // categories on Snowdon Towers (air terminals from the IFC4 subtype of
     // the IFC2x3 `IfcFlowTerminal` Revit writes on RE1 Mechanical).
